module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_killed_serve_process_holds_its_ledger_charge_until_the_lease_lapses

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode, BelowCeiling, FoundOnRung, RungRead }
import gunbc.guarantee_rung { Mitigatable, StructurallyImpossible }
import gunbc.rung_climb { AwaitsOneGrounding, GroundingUndeclared }

data a_killed_serve_process_holds_its_ledger_charge_until_the_lease_lapses: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  // THE NAME SAYS "UNTIL THE LEASE LAPSES" AND THE MODEL SAYS OTHERWISE; the name is kept as the state
  // was first believed, and the receipts below carry the correction, because the correction is the
  // useful part. A compute seat is QUIESCENCE-REQUIRED, so no lapse releases it (see FOUND).
  identity: "a_killed_serve_process_holds_its_ledger_charge_until_the_lease_lapses" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungRead {
    found_at: FoundOnRung { rung: Mitigatable },
    standing: BelowCeiling {
      current: FoundOnRung { rung: Mitigatable },
      ceiling: StructurallyImpossible,
      blocker: AwaitsOneGrounding { grounding: GroundingUndeclared },
      trigger_capability: "the remote-build serving path settles the attempt when its holder dies -- the serve writing its own ending on exit, and the host's supervisor settling a unit that died by signal -- so the charge's lifetime is carried by the same object that carries the bound it reserves, and the belt's ensure pass becomes a backstop for a host that was unreachable while the work ran rather than the only route back"
    }
  },

  receipts: [
    "**INVALID STATE: a compute reservation is charged to a host's capacity ledger under an ATTEMPT REFERENCE, and the only thing that ends the charge is an explicit release -- a settle the holder writes, or an ensure that re-proves the holder's attempt ended. Nothing notices that the process the charge was made for is gone. A process killed by signal -- not ended, not settled -- leaves its charge standing, and the ledger has no field distinguishing a charge whose holder runs from a charge whose holder is a corpse.**",

    "THE BOUND IS NOT THE LEASE -- THAT WAS THE FIRST READING OF THIS ROW, AND IT IS WRONG, AND THE CORRECTION IS WHY THE ROW IS WORTH KEEPING. A compute seat is declared quiescence-required (gunbc.compute.host_capacity compute_lease_policy: LeasePolicy { maximum_duration_seconds, release_law: QuiescenceRequired }), and product.capacity.pool lapse_pass releases NOTHING for such a pool -- `QuiescenceRequired => LapsePass { pool, lapsed: [] }` -- because the automatic lapse belongs to FENCED resources, where the resource itself refuses the stale holder. host_capacity says the consequence plainly: a term that runs out frees nothing; the pool holds the encumbrance until the seat is RELEASED; a worker that dies wedges its own share rather than handing it to the next arrival, which is the only direction that cannot oversubscribe a machine. What the term DOES bound is the holder's own authority: after it lapses, the holder is refused with LeaseAlreadyLapsed while the charge stands.",

    "FOUND: 2026-11-20, srv1, on the remote-build serving entry. Around 01:20Z a ctrl worker's `pgrep -x claim_batch` sweep killed srv1 PIDs 1821238 and 1821317 with SIGKILL; one was a cargo build under the remote-build serve, so its serve process died holding a build-pool reservation. By 06:35Z that host had answered CAPACITY-FULL for over two hours under one identity (`build:release:gunbc,claim_batch`, reference 09bcdd84...), four identical answers observed by a sibling lane -- longer than the 7200s term, exactly as the quiescence-required law predicts, and not evidence that a lapse failed to fold: no lapse was expected to free it.",

    "HARM, AND ITS SIZE IS THE RECOVERY CADENCE, NOT A CONSTANT. On a host whose whole build-pool appropriation is one CI runner slot, one wedged seat means EVERY later request is refused capacity-full -- the remote-build client reports that as exit 3 'no host admitted', and the required gate cannot run at all -- until something releases. Two facts bound that: the in-corpus discharge door exists (gunbc.compute.compute_seat_ensure compute_seat_ensure_in, driven from the belt's compute_seat_discharge_all), and it only acts on a PROVEN ended attempt (LoadState=not-found, or the --wait launcher having returned success), refusing and KEEPING the charge when the slot is unreadable, absent, or the lifecycle unknown. So the outage is bounded by the belt's cadence plus a lifecycle that can be established, and unbounded wherever that pass does not run. The readback is what makes the door trustworthy: a release counts only when the pool's committed figure fell by exactly the attempt's grant (SeatEnsureRed otherwise), because a settle that reports without the committed figure falling is the same failure in a different costume (RecoverySettledButHeld).",

    "DISTINGUISHING FACTS: the charge is a row in a table keyed by an attempt reference, while the LIMIT it stands for is a kernel object (the unit's memory.max) that dies with its holder -- enforcement is already self-clearing and only the ACCOUNTING outlives the work; a lapse is an EVENT in this ledger rather than a comparison done at read time, so 'the fold did not apply PoolLapsed' cannot be the explanation when the lapse pass is a deliberate no-op for this pool; and there is a capacity reader (compute_capacity_standing_on) but no SURFACE that reports it -- fleet.host_capacity_publish has no consumer -- so the wedged share is invisible until a requester trips over it.",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: mitigatable -- the door discharges it and demands readback, so a wedge is recoverable without inventing a new release path. CURRENT RUNG: mitigatable, by that door. CEILING: structurally impossible -- once the charge's lifetime is carried by the same object that carries the bound it reserves (the attempt's unit, or a settle written by whoever supervises that unit's death), a holder that dies cannot leave a charge behind, and the table can no longer disagree with the kernel. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, named so a narrower repair cannot satisfy it: THE SERVING PATH SETTLES THE ATTEMPT WHEN ITS HOLDER DIES -- the serve writing its own ending on exit AND the host's supervisor settling a unit that died by signal. Naming 'run the ensure more often' as the trigger would satisfy itself while the class stayed alive everywhere else, because shortening the detection interval is not a rule that ends the state.",

    "RECOGNITION RULE, ONE QUESTION: for every resource this corpus charges to a table, name the object that ENFORCES that charge, and ask what happens to the table entry when the enforcing object disappears on its own. If nothing notices, the row is in this class -- and its size is the recovery cadence, not the timeout written next to the entry.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
